{#
   CS 301R — the checkpoint rubrics. THE SOURCE OF TRUTH for every grid.

   Everything rubric-shaped is generated from this file:

     Canvas rubric objects   canvas_material/deploy_rubrics.py (attached to each
                             checkpoint, used for grading in SpeedGrader)
     Assignment Rubrics page pages/rubrics.md.jinja (pg-rubrics)
     Handout grading tables  the "What it measures" table in each CP handout,
                             via macros.jinja → rubric_summary()
     Instructor view         rubrics.md at the repo root, regenerated with
                             `python3 canvas_material/deploy_rubrics.py instructor-md`

   Edit here, never in those outputs.

   This file is Jinja-rendered with the target's global args before it is
   parsed as YAML, so a date is written {{ S08_DATE }}, never literally.

   Per rubric:
     title, subtitle   heading and italic line on the rubrics page
     phase             CPS phase heading in the instructor view
     publish           true once the grid has been reviewed in student-facing
                       wording. Gates the rubrics page AND the Canvas rubric.
                       Flip it in the same pass as the assignment's Publish
                       column in checkpoints-args.md.jinja.
     criteria          name, weight (weights sum to 100), measures (one line,
                       student-facing: the handout table and the Canvas
                       criterion description), levels (the four-level grid)
     looking_for       student-facing "what each criterion is looking for"
     instructor_notes  instructor-only commentary; appears in rubrics.md only

   Field text is Markdown. <course-link> tags are fine; the Canvas deploy
   strips them (and Markdown emphasis) to plain text.
#}
scale:
  - level: Exemplary
    points: 4
    meaning: Professional quality. Could serve as a model for the class.
  - level: Proficient
    points: 3
    meaning: Solid and complete; meets the founder-grade bar.
  - level: Developing
    points: 2
    meaning: Present but thin, uneven, or partly missing. Needs another pass.
  - level: Beginning
    points: 1
    meaning: Missing, superficial, or seriously flawed.
  - level: Absent
    points: 0
    meaning: Not submitted; no evidence.

phases:
  1: Phase 1 — Clarify & Explore
  2: Phase 2 — Ideate & Pitch → Form Teams
  3: Phase 3 — Develop & Design
  4: Phase 4 — Implement & Build
  5: Phase 5 — Launch & Reflect

rubrics:

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------- CP1
  cp1:
    title: CP1 — Idea Briefs
    subtitle: Three one-page candidate concepts · Individual · Part of the 10% Discovery bundle, with CP2
    phase: 1
    publish: true
    criteria:
      - name: Divergence & breadth
        weight: 25
        measures: Three genuinely distinct problem spaces spanning different users/domains — evidence of real divergent exploration, not one idea in three costumes.
        levels:
          exemplary: Three genuinely distinct problem spaces spanning different users/domains
          proficient: Three distinct ideas, some overlap
          developing: Ideas are minor variants of one another
          beginning: Effectively one idea, or fewer than three
      - name: Real-need orientation
        weight: 25
        measures: Each idea names a plausible real user and a felt need, with at least a hint of evidence. Keeps the focus on serving someone.
        levels:
          exemplary: Each names a plausible real user and a felt need, with a hint of evidence
          proficient: Each names a user and need
          developing: Needs asserted but vague or self-serving
          beginning: Toy/hypothetical needs only
      - name: Richness potential
        weight: 20
        measures: At least one idea visibly could grow into a multi-dimensional, lasting project.
        levels:
          exemplary: At least one idea visibly could grow into a multi-dimensional, lasting project
          proficient: One idea shows some breadth
          developing: Breadth unclear for all three
          beginning: All ideas trivial in scope
      - name: Clarity & concision
        weight: 15
        measures: Crisp, one-page discipline; ideas are easy to grasp quickly.
        levels:
          exemplary: Crisp, one-page discipline; easy to grasp fast
          proficient: Readable, mostly to length
          developing: Padded or hard to follow
          beginning: Disorganized or incomplete
      - name: Reflection
        weight: 15
        measures: A thoughtful, personal take on what makes a project worth building to last — not a generic summary.
        levels:
          exemplary: Thoughtful take on what makes a project worth building to last
          proficient: Reasonable reflection
          developing: Generic reflection
          beginning: Missing or perfunctory
    looking_for:
      - "**Divergence & breadth** rewards the divergent-thinking discipline the whole first phase depends on: explore *across* the problem space rather than refining one pet idea."
      - "**Real-need orientation** keeps the focus on real users from day one. The course is selecting for projects that serve someone, not clever toys."
      - "**Richness potential** asks whether a team could grow into this — the \"is it worth founding on?\" question, applied gently at the idea stage."
      - "**Clarity & concision** enforces the one-page constraint. Founders have to communicate an idea fast."
      - "**Reflection** assesses whether you have internalized the founding mindset, not just produced ideas."
    instructor_notes:
      - "**Divergence & breadth** rewards the divergent-thinking discipline the CPS spine depends on early: students should explore *across* the problem space, not refine one pet idea."
      - "**Richness potential** is the \"could a multi-disciplinary team grow into this?\" north star (§9), applied gently at the idea stage."

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------- CP2
  cp2:
    title: CP2 — Product Definition Brief
    subtitle: Users, needs, success criteria · Individual · Completes the 10% Discovery bundle
    phase: 1
    publish: true
    criteria:
      - name: Target user clarity
        weight: 20
        measures: Userbase sharply defined and bounded — who's in and out is clear. Not "everyone."
        levels:
          exemplary: Userbase sharply defined and bounded; who's in and out is clear
          proficient: User defined
          developing: User vague or overbroad
          beginning: '"Everyone" / undefined'
      - name: Needfinding quality & honesty
        weight: 30
        measures: Real interviews/observation **and/or** a rigorously documented persona — ideally interviews *informing* a persona — each with honest reasoning about its limits. **Honesty about method is graded as heavily as the findings.**
        levels:
          exemplary: Real interviews/observation **and/or** a rigorously documented persona or proxy — ideally interviews informing a persona — with honest reasoning about its limits
          proficient: Credible needfinding with some evidence
          developing: Thin evidence; reasoning gaps
          beginning: Asserted needs, no method
      - name: Prioritized needs
        weight: 20
        measures: Needs prioritized (must-have vs. nice-to-have) with a defensible rationale.
        levels:
          exemplary: Needs prioritized with a defensible rationale
          proficient: Needs listed and ranked
          developing: Needs listed, not prioritized
          beginning: Needs unclear
      - name: Success criteria
        weight: 20
        measures: Specific, observable, defensible criteria for "this works."
        levels:
          exemplary: Specific, defensible, observable criteria for "this works"
          proficient: Reasonable criteria
          developing: Vague criteria
          beginning: Missing
      - name: PRD mapping
        weight: 10
        measures: Reads like a lightweight product-requirements doc (see the <course-link type="page" id="pg-prd-reference">PRD reference</course-link>) — a clear definition of problem and user, not a solution.
        levels:
          exemplary: Cleanly reads as a real PRD — problem and user, not solution
          proficient: Mostly reads as a PRD
          developing: Partial
          beginning: Not recognizable as a PRD
    looking_for:
      - "**Target user clarity** — a founder has to know exactly whose problem they own. Fuzziness here undermines everything downstream: you cannot scope an MVP, argue feasibility, or judge whether you succeeded."
      - "**Needfinding quality & honesty** is the heart of the discovery grade. Because an evidence-tagged persona is an accepted substitute when you genuinely cannot reach a user, *honesty about your method and its limits* is graded as heavily as the findings themselves. A brief built largely on assumptions can still score well if it is straight about that and clear about what it would verify."
      - "**Prioritized needs** checks that you can tell must-haves from nice-to-haves. That distinction is the basis of every later scoping decision."
      - "**Success criteria** force a falsifiable definition of success — the one your final demo gets judged against."
      - "**PRD mapping** ties this to the shape the document takes in industry. See the <course-link type=\"page\" id=\"pg-prd-reference\">PRD reference</course-link>."
    instructor_notes:
      - "**Target user clarity** — a founder/sponsor must know exactly whose problem they own; fuzziness here undermines everything downstream."
      - "**Needfinding quality & honesty** — persona/proxy work is an allowed substitute this iteration, so honesty about method and its limits is graded as heavily as the findings."
      - "**PRD mapping** ties this artifact to the sponsor role the lead may carry into the capstone."

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------- CP3
  cp3:
    title: CP3 — Written Proposal
    subtitle: "Individual · Course weight: 12%"
    phase: 2
    publish: true
    criteria:
      - name: Problem & user clarity
        weight: 15
        measures: Problem and users compelling and evidence-backed — the discovery trail shows.
        levels:
          exemplary: Problem and users compelling and evidence-backed
          proficient: Clear problem and users
          developing: Somewhat vague
          beginning: Unclear
      - name: Solution & core features (+ non-goals)
        weight: 15
        measures: Coherent solution; sharp, prioritized feature set; explicit non-goals. Scoping *out* is as persuasive as scoping in.
        levels:
          exemplary: Coherent solution; sharp feature set; explicit non-goals
          proficient: Solution and features defined
          developing: Features fuzzy; no non-goals
          beginning: Solution unclear
      - name: Feasibility & technical approach
        weight: 20
        measures: Stack and dependencies actually researched; **at least one spike run on the scariest unknown, with its result honestly reported**; approach credible and justified — real homework, not hand-waving.
        levels:
          exemplary: Stack and dependencies researched; approach credible and justified
          proficient: Reasonable, mostly justified
          developing: Approach asserted, thin
          beginning: Infeasible or absent
      - name: Scope realism (MVP for this term)
        weight: 15
        measures: A clear, achievable vertical slice, sized against the *real* build window — roughly five weeks, with the ~10 hrs/week total already spent on class, reading, writing, and reviews — with the arithmetic shown.
        levels:
          exemplary: Clear, achievable vertical slice, sized against the *real* build window — roughly five weeks — with the arithmetic shown
          proficient: Plausible scope
          developing: Over- or under-scoped
          beginning: Wildly unrealistic (e.g. "10 hrs × 14 weeks = 140 hours")
      - name: Richness & extensibility
        weight: 15
        measures: Exercises multiple engineering dimensions; a path for others to join and build.
        levels:
          exemplary: Exercises multiple engineering dimensions; clear path for a larger team
          proficient: Some breadth and extensibility
          developing: Narrow
          beginning: Toy-scale
      - name: Risks & mitigation
        weight: 10
        measures: Real, specific risks with credible mitigations — engineering judgment about what could go wrong.
        levels:
          exemplary: Real risks named with credible mitigations
          proficient: Risks identified
          developing: Generic risks
          beginning: None
      - name: Argument & revision
        weight: 10
        measures: Persuasive, well-written, and visibly improved in response to peer feedback — measured against your {{ S08_DATE }} draft and the annotated copies you submit with it. **No draft on file caps this criterion at Developing.**
        levels:
          exemplary: Persuasive, well-written; visible response to peer feedback
          proficient: Clear writing; some revision
          developing: Rough; little revision
          beginning: Poor or no revision
    looking_for:
      - "**Problem & user clarity** and **Solution & core features** carry the product case. **Non-goals** are graded because scoping something *out* is as persuasive as scoping it in."
      - "**Feasibility & technical approach** is the heaviest criterion here, deliberately. It rewards real homework on stack and dependencies — arguing feasibility without overpromising is the skill this genre lives or dies on."
      - "**Scope realism** is checked against the honest budget: **~10 hrs/week is the total course load** — class, reading, discovery, writing, and reviews all come out of it — so the build time left is a fraction of ten, spread over roughly **five weeks** of real building. A proposal that sizes its MVP by multiplying 10 × 14 has already failed this criterion."
      - "**Richness & extensibility** applies the same \"worth founding on?\" test your pitch will be judged on."
      - "**Risks & mitigation** assesses engineering judgment about what can go wrong, and whether your mitigations are real."
      - "**Argument & revision** grades the proposal as technical communication, and rewards iteration. **It requires a draft on file.** The <course-link type=\"assignment\" id=\"cp3-draft\">required draft</course-link> — uploaded, plus two printed copies marked up in class and submitted as images with the final — is what revision is measured against. **With no draft on file this criterion caps at Developing**, because there is nothing to measure improvement against."
    instructor_notes:
      - "**Scope realism** — the build window is roughly five weeks (Weeks 9–13), not the nominal ~10 hrs/week × 14."
      - "**Argument & revision** requires a draft on file: the {{ S08_DATE }} workshop draft (uploaded, two printed copies marked up in class, scans submitted with the final). **With no draft on file this criterion caps at Developing** — Canvas will not enforce the cap; apply it when scoring."
      - "**Richness & extensibility** applies the §9 selection criteria the proposal will be judged on at pitch time."

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------- CP4
  cp4:
    title: CP4 — Pitch Presentation
    subtitle: "Persuade and recruit · Individual · Course weight: 8%"
    phase: 2
    publish: true
    criteria:
      - name: Problem/solution clarity
        weight: 20
        measures: The audience grasps the need and the idea immediately — no rewinding.
        levels:
          exemplary: Audience grasps the need and idea immediately
          proficient: Clear
          developing: Somewhat muddled
          beginning: Confusing
      - name: Persuasiveness & recruiting
        weight: 25
        measures: Compelling enough to attract teammates, with a clear call to join. Weighted highest because the pitch's *job* is to recruit — audience effect is part of the grade.
        levels:
          exemplary: Compelling enough to attract teammates; clear call to join
          proficient: Persuasive
          developing: Flat
          beginning: Unconvincing
      - name: Feasibility defense
        weight: 15
        measures: Scope and tradeoffs defended convincingly — the <course-link type="assignment" id="cp3">CP3</course-link> honest-confidence move, spoken.
        levels:
          exemplary: Defends scope and tradeoffs convincingly
          proficient: Defends adequately
          developing: Shaky
          beginning: Cannot defend
      - name: Handling of questions
        weight: 15
        measures: Direct, composed, honest under questioning — including honest "I don't know yet"s.
        levels:
          exemplary: Direct, composed, honest under questioning
          proficient: Handles most questions
          developing: Struggles
          beginning: Deflects or unable
      - name: Slide & visual quality
        weight: 10
        measures: Few, clean, purposeful visuals that help rather than compete.
        levels:
          exemplary: Clean, purposeful visuals
          proficient: Serviceable
          developing: Cluttered
          beginning: Poor or absent
      - name: Delivery & professionalism
        weight: 15
        measures: Confident, well-paced, within time.
        levels:
          exemplary: Confident, well-paced, within time
          proficient: Solid delivery
          developing: Uneven or over time
          beginning: Unprofessional
    looking_for:
      - "**Persuasiveness & recruiting** is weighted highest because of what Pitch Day is for: a pitch *succeeds* if it convinces classmates to spend their semester on your project. This is the one rubric in the course where the effect on your audience is part of the grade."
      - "**Feasibility defense** and **Handling of questions** test whether you can think on your feet about your own project's risks. Honest answers score better than confident ones."
      - "**Slide & visual quality** and **Delivery** assess pitch craft as a professional communication skill."
      - "Peer scores from the <course-link type=\"page\" id=\"pg-pitch-evaluation-sheet\">pitch evaluation sheet</course-link> inform the instructor's judgment here, but the grade is the instructor's."
    instructor_notes:
      - "**Persuasiveness & recruiting** is weighted highest because Week 5 is the course hinge."

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------- CP5
  cp5:
    title: CP5 — Design Document
    subtitle: "How it gets built, and why this way · Team (or solo, at solo scope) · Course weight: 12%"
    phase: 3
    publish: true
    criteria:
      - name: Architecture clarity
        weight: 20
        measures: Components, interfaces, data, and external services clearly modeled — the system's shape is visible.
        levels:
          exemplary: Components, interfaces, data, and external services clearly modeled — the system's shape is visible
          proficient: Architecture clear
          developing: Partial or ambiguous
          beginning: Missing or incoherent
      - name: Appropriate detail
        weight: 15
        measures: "The right altitude: neither hand-wavy nor over-specified. A 300-level judgment skill in its own right."
        levels:
          exemplary: Right altitude — neither hand-wavy nor over-specified
          proficient: Mostly right
          developing: Too shallow or too deep
          beginning: Unusable
      - name: Tech choices & rationale
        weight: 15
        measures: Choices justified against real alternatives — the alternatives-considered discipline.
        levels:
          exemplary: Each significant choice argued against a real alternative
          proficient: Choices justified
          developing: Asserted, little rationale
          beginning: Unjustified
      - name: Data & interface design
        weight: 15
        measures: A sound data model and interface sketch that the traced flow actually exercises.
        levels:
          exemplary: Sound data model and interface sketch that the traced flow exercises
          proficient: Present and reasonable
          developing: Incomplete
          beginning: Missing
      - name: Risk & open questions
        weight: 10
        measures: Honest unknowns surfaced — including failure cases — rather than hidden.
        levels:
          exemplary: Honest risks and open questions surfaced, including failure cases
          proficient: Some risks noted
          developing: Token
          beginning: None
      - name: Implementability
        weight: 15
        measures: The plan is clearly buildable in the semester that remains, by this team.
        levels:
          exemplary: Plan clearly buildable in the semester that remains, by this team
          proficient: Buildable
          developing: Doubtful
          beginning: Not actionable
      - name: Readability & revision
        weight: 10
        measures: A future contributor could navigate it; visible improvement from draft to final.
        levels:
          exemplary: A future contributor could navigate it; visible improvement from draft to final
          proficient: Readable; some revision
          developing: Rough
          beginning: Poor or no revision
    looking_for:
      - "**Architecture clarity**, **Data & interface design**, and **Tech choices & rationale** are the engineering core: can your team show the shape of the system and defend the decisions that produced it. A straw-man alternative fools no one — the alternative has to be something a reasonable team might actually pick."
      - "**Appropriate detail** is a judgment skill in its own right, and the one this assignment is really teaching. Decisions and trade-offs belong in the document; mechanics belong in the code. Copied schemas, pseudo-code for routine work, and pinned library versions cost you here."
      - "**Implementability** ties the design to the reality of the build: roughly five weeks of real building, not a nominal semester."
      - "**Readability & revision** is graded through the future-contributor lens the whole course runs on — plus visible improvement between the draft and the final. Revision is graded, so keep the draft."
      - "Use the <course-link type=\"page\" id=\"pg-design-doc-template\">design document template</course-link>: its sections map onto these criteria one for one."
    instructor_notes:
      - "**Appropriate detail** is a 300-level judgment skill: documenting enough without over-specifying."
      - "**Readability** is graded through the *future-contributor* lens central to the course — the doc must serve a stranger who joins the project."
      - "Team grade, adjusted by individual-contribution evidence (design §8)."

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------- CP6
  cp6:
    title: CP6 — Git Workflow Lab
    subtitle: Team (or solo) · Part of the 12% Git + Tech-Comm bundle, with CP9
    phase: 3
    publish: true
    criteria:
      - name: Commit hygiene
        weight: 25
        measures: Logical, atomic commits; clear, conventional messages in the imperative that explain why.
        levels:
          exemplary: Logical, atomic commits; messages pass the seven rules and explain why
          proficient: Mostly clean; messages say what changed
          developing: Noisy or uneven; "WIP"/"fixed stuff" in the history
          beginning: Dump commits; messages explain nothing
      - name: Branch & PR workflow
        weight: 25
        measures: "The maintainer-centered flow followed correctly: branch → PR → review → merge under policy."
        levels:
          exemplary: Issue → branch → PR → review → merge under policy, every step visible; conflict resolved in its PR
          proficient: Workflow followed, minor gaps
          developing: Steps skipped or out of order
          beginning: Not followed
      - name: PR quality
        weight: 20
        measures: Scoped, reviewable PRs with descriptions that orient the reviewer.
        levels:
          exemplary: Small, scoped PRs; description gives problem, approach, and where to look first, and closes the issue
          proficient: Reasonable PRs with a real description
          developing: Large or unclear; thin description
          beginning: Unreviewable
      - name: Review participation
        weight: 20
        measures: Substantive, constructive written review of a peer's PR — a graded skill, not a formality.
        levels:
          exemplary: Substantive, kind review of another maintainer's PR; every comment on your own PR answered
          proficient: Some real review; responses to most comments
          developing: Rubber-stamp ("LGTM" alone)
          beginning: None
      - name: Project git standards
        weight: 10
        measures: Standards documented, reasoned, and actually followed in the lab itself.
        levels:
          exemplary: Each standard documented with its rationale, and the lab's own history follows it
          proficient: Documented
          developing: Vague, or contradicted by the history
          beginning: Absent
    looking_for:
      - "Everything is read from the repository — its issues, pull requests, reviews, and history. That is how a project's engineering is judged in the world, too: a future contributor or a hiring manager opens the repo, not a write-up about it."
      - "**Commit hygiene** and **PR quality** are the record you leave for the next reader. A message that explains *why*, and a PR small enough to actually review, are what make that record useful."
      - "**Branch & PR workflow** is about each step being visible, including the merge conflict resolved inside its PR. Rewriting a branch before it merges (force-pushing over commits your reviewer read) erases the evidence; squash-merging afterward does not."
      - "**Review participation** is weighted as heavily as PR quality because reviewing someone else's code well is a skill in its own right. \"LGTM\" with nothing else is a rubber stamp; a real question counts as substance. Answering the comments on your own PR counts here too."
      - "**Project git standards** asks whether your project wrote its own norms, gave a reason for each, and then actually followed them."
    instructor_notes:
      - "This lab grades *mechanics under a real workflow*, not git trivia. The emphasis is the **maintainer-centered** model (branch → logical commits → draft PR → review → merge under policy), not fork-and-upstream."
      - "**Review participation** is weighted because reviewing others' code well is a graded skill in its own right, not a formality."
      - "**Project git standards** rewards setting and honoring the project's own conventions — founders write the norms."

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------- CP7
  cp7:
    title: CP7 — Project Infrastructure Package
    subtitle: "The front door a stranger walks through · Team (or solo) · Course weight: 12%"
    phase: 3
    publish: true
    criteria:
      - name: Completeness
        weight: 25
        measures: All eight front-door items are in the repository and each one does its job, not just exists.
        levels:
          exemplary: All eight items present and substantive — README, LICENSE, CONTRIBUTING, code of conduct, templates + labels, setup placeholder, communication norms, seed issues
          proficient: All present; one or two thin
          developing: One or two missing, or several present in name only
          beginning: Most missing or skeletal
      - name: Newcomer usability
        weight: 25
        measures: From the README and CONTRIBUTING alone, a stranger can tell what the project is, what works today, where it's going, and how to start helping.
        levels:
          exemplary: A stranger can say what it is, its honest status, and their first step from the docs alone; the partner's cold read was acted on
          proficient: Mostly self-serve; one question a stranger would still have to ask
          developing: The first sentence or the first step is unclear; a stranger needs the author
          beginning: A stranger can't tell what this is or how to help
      - name: License appropriateness
        weight: 15
        measures: The exact license text, correctly filled in, with a recorded rationale for why it fits this project's goals.
        levels:
          exemplary: Exact text, fields correct, and a rationale that argues this license for this project against a real alternative
          proficient: Correct license with a reasonable rationale
          developing: License present but unjustified, or fields wrong
          beginning: Missing, or a poor fit for the project's stated goals
      - name: Governance & comms norms
        weight: 15
        measures: A contributor knows where to talk, how fast to expect a reply, who decides, and how to report a problem — and every promise is one you can keep.
        levels:
          exemplary: Channels, keepable response times, who maintains, how decisions get made, and a working conduct-reporting contact
          proficient: All present; one vague or optimistic
          developing: Several missing, or promises no student project can keep
          beginning: Absent
      - name: Professionalism & consistency
        weight: 10
        measures: The files agree with each other and the repository, links work, and everything shipped through reviewed pull requests.
        levels:
          exemplary: Consistent names, promises, and links across every file; every item merged through a reviewed PR
          proficient: Minor inconsistencies or a broken link
          developing: Files contradict each other, or pushed straight to main
          beginning: Sloppy; reads as unfinished
      - name: Contributor-readiness
        weight: 10
        measures: Templates, labels, and seed issues make a good contribution the easy one to make.
        levels:
          exemplary: Templates ask for what a maintainer needs; labels support triage; 2–3 real seed issues written to the templates' own standard
          proficient: Templates and labels in place; seed issues adequate
          developing: Stock templates unadapted, or seed issues vague
          beginning: No templates or seed issues
    looking_for:
      - "Everything is read from the repository, the way a stranger meets it: logged out, with no one to ask. That's how a potential contributor, a user, or an employer meets any project in the world."
      - "**Completeness** and **Newcomer usability** carry half the points between them. Completeness asks whether each of the eight items is there and doing its job; Newcomer usability asks whether the README and CONTRIBUTING actually orient someone. The README's first sentence and an honest status line matter more than length. **Someone else has to read it cold:** a front door no one has tested on a stranger tops out at Proficient here *(in this course, your partner's Stranger's Read sheet, photographed and submitted with CP7)*."
      - "**A project that doesn't run yet should say so.** \"Not runnable yet — see `docs/setup.md`\", with a placeholder that names the stack and links the issue tracking the real guide, earns full credit. A quickstart that fails costs you under both Newcomer usability and Professionalism. *(Install and run are graded later, in the repo audit, once there's code to set up.)*"
      - "**License appropriateness** rewards a reasoned choice, not a particular license. MIT can score Exemplary if the rationale argues it for your project's goals; any license scores poorly if the only reason is that it's the default."
      - "**Governance & comms norms** is graded on credibility. \"Reviews within a week\" from a one-person student project is a promise a contributor can rely on; \"24/7 support\" is not. Solo founders still owe an answer to \"how do decisions get made?\": how does someone else's proposal get decided, and where?"
      - "**Professionalism & consistency** catches the front door that contradicts itself: a README promising Discord when the norms say issues only, a CONTRIBUTING that links a file that isn't there. Shipping every item through a pull request is part of it: the process documents itself."
      - "**Contributor-readiness** asks whether your templates, labels, and seed issues make the right contribution the easy one. Whatever a template asks for is what you'll get, so adapt the stock forms to what you actually need."
    instructor_notes:
      - "**Completeness** maps to the front-door subset of design §10's required repository artifacts. The setup guide is a *placeholder* in CP7 (stack + tracking issue); the full guide is written and partner-cold-tested in Session 20 and followed by the Session 22 auditor."
      - "**Newcomer usability** does *not* grade install/run — that's CP10's *Repo audit* criterion. Evidence for the partner read: the photo of the Stranger's Read sheet submitted with CP7, and PRs/issues that cite it. Grade the repo at the `cp7` tag, not at main. A missing read caps this criterion at Proficient — but only for a founder who skipped it: if a partner is absent, the instructor reads, and an odd count makes a triple that rotates (each person reads one repo and is read by one)."
      - "**Governance & comms norms** is the founder-defining-the-rules emphasis: the public parts of the team charter, rewritten for strangers. The code of conduct's reporting contact belongs here, not under Completeness."
      - "**Contributor-readiness** is graded on the machinery (templates, labels, seed issues); the *welcome* lives in Newcomer usability via CONTRIBUTING. Keep them separate when scoring so one weak file isn't penalized twice."
      - "One grade per project; on team projects, adjusted by individual contribution (design §8)."

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------- CP8
  cp8:
    title: CP8 — Project Plan & Roadmap
    subtitle: "Your plan, in public · Team (or solo) · Course weight: 8%"
    phase: 4
    publish: true
    criteria:
      - name: Work breakdown
        weight: 25
        measures: The MVP is cut into epics of user-visible value and issues someone could pick up, each with acceptance criteria, grouped into dated milestones on a public board that shows real state.
        levels:
          exemplary: Epics named for value; every M1 issue one coherent, reviewable change with checkable acceptance criteria; M2 coarse but real; seed issues triaged; the board is public and true
          proficient: A sound breakdown; a few issues too big, too vague, or missing acceptance criteria
          developing: Epics that are really components, or issues without acceptance criteria; the board out of date or private
          beginning: A list of features, not a plan someone could work from
      - name: Prioritization & estimation
        weight: 20
        measures: The backlog is in order, every M1 issue is sized, and the hours were counted — M1 fits the time that actually exists, or something was cut.
        levels:
          exemplary: Ordered with the slice and the riskiest work first; every M1 issue sized; the capacity arithmetic shown, and M1 cut to fit
          proficient: Ordered and sized; capacity checked loosely
          developing: Some order or some sizes; no capacity check
          beginning: No order and no sizes
      - name: Definition of done
        weight: 15
        measures: The project-wide bar every change must pass, revisited in your git standards now that there's code, so each item can be checked.
        levels:
          exemplary: Every item checkable (the test command named, what "docs updated" means, who confirms); linked from CONTRIBUTING
          proficient: Revisited and mostly checkable; one item vague
          developing: Unchanged since CP6, or mostly items no one could check
          beginning: Absent
      - name: Contribution pathways
        weight: 20
        measures: A newcomer can find a real place to start, and has what they need to start it without asking.
        levels:
          exemplary: 2–3 genuinely small starting-point issues, each with a *Where to start*; labels in use and explained in CONTRIBUTING; the board and roadmap linked from the front door
          proficient: Starting points marked; one thin on context
          developing: Marked but not approachable, or no context to start from
          beginning: No way in for a newcomer
      - name: Roadmap into the future
        weight: 20
        measures: Where the project goes after the MVP, as others join — written down, honest about what was cut, and framed as places to help.
        levels:
          exemplary: Next and later work in order, with what was cut and why; each item framed as a place to help; non-goals stated
          proficient: A credible roadmap; thin on what was cut or where others fit
          developing: A wish list with no order or reasoning
          beginning: Missing
    looking_for:
      - "Everything is read from the repository and the board, logged out, the way a would-be contributor meets a project: no spreadsheet, no explanation from you. A private board can't be graded, so check its visibility."
      - "**The defaults are defaults.** A task form, an `epic` label, the five board columns, and a `needs triage` label are one good way to do each job, not the only one. Changing any of them costs nothing here, provided CONTRIBUTING says what you do instead and why. A board with no review state is the exception: every project works through pull requests, so keep one."
      - "**Work breakdown** is graded on the issues as much as the epics. The test for an issue is whether it's one coherent change one person could carry and a reviewer could review as a unit, and whether its acceptance criteria are checkable: \"checkout works\" is a hope, \"an item that's already out can't be checked out again\" is a criterion. Detail M1 fully and M2 loosely; that's the right shape, not a shortcut."
      - "**Prioritization & estimation** rewards honesty over ambition. Show the capacity arithmetic (who, how many hours, how many weeks) and what you cut to make M1 fit. An M1 that was trimmed scores higher than an impressive one that couldn't fit."
      - "**Definition of done** lives in your git standards from CP6; don't copy it into a new file. It's graded on whether a reviewer could check each item, now that there's code to test."
      - "**Contribution pathways** and **Roadmap into the future** carry 40% together, on purpose: a plan that only serves the people building it today is half a founding artifact. A starting-point issue with no context is a trap, not an invitation. The roadmap is allowed to dream, as long as dreams are labeled as dreams."
    instructor_notes:
      - "Grade the repository and the public board as of the due date; the submission is the board URL. If the board is private, ask for it to be made public before grading rather than scoring it as missing."
      - "**Work breakdown** and **Prioritization & estimation** assess turning vision into manageable, sequenced work. Sizes are taught in Session 18 (t-shirt + \"does M1 fit?\"); accept any consistent scheme. The slice statement on M1 belongs here and becomes CP10's commitment."
      - "**Departures from the defaults** (task form, `epic` label, columns, intake mechanism) are never a deduction when CONTRIBUTING gives the reason; an unexplained gap is graded as a gap. Only the review state on the board is fixed."
      - "**Definition of done** stays a section of `docs/git-standards.md` (decided Oct 2026; v4 §10 updated). Grade the revision since CP6, not the existence of a file."
      - "**Contribution pathways** and **Roadmap into the future** are weighted heavily because *creating on-ramps for future contributors* is a defining course goal, not an afterthought."
      - "One grade per project; on team projects, adjusted by individual contribution (design §8)."

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------- CP9
  cp9:
    title: CP9 — Technical Communication Portfolio
    subtitle: Individual · Completes the 12% Git + Tech-Comm bundle, with CP6
    phase: 4
    publish: true
    criteria:
      - name: Precision & clarity
        weight: 25
        measures: Exact and unambiguous — the reader knows precisely what's meant on the first pass.
        levels:
          exemplary: Exact and unambiguous throughout; the reader never has to guess what's meant
          proficient: Clear, with an occasional loose phrase
          developing: Some ambiguity a reader would have to ask about
          beginning: Vague or confusing
      - name: Completeness
        weight: 25
        measures: Bug reports carry steps to reproduce and expected vs. actual; feature requests state the need, the user, and the workaround.
        levels:
          exemplary: Every bug report has the full anatomy; the feature request frames the need, the user, and the workaround
          proficient: Mostly complete; one part thin
          developing: Key parts missing, such as expected vs. actual or the environment
          beginning: Incomplete throughout
      - name: Actionability
        weight: 20
        measures: A maintainer could act immediately — no follow-up questions required.
        levels:
          exemplary: A maintainer could act on each artifact immediately
          proficient: Actionable, with a question or two
          developing: Needs a round of follow-up before anyone can act
          beginning: Not actionable
      - name: Tone & professionalism
        weight: 15
        measures: Respectful and collaborative — even, especially, when critical.
        levels:
          exemplary: Respectful and collaborative, including when critical; on the work, not the person
          proficient: Professional
          developing: Uneven; curt or careless in places
          beginning: Unprofessional
      - name: Responsiveness to feedback
        weight: 15
        measures: Critique engaged thoughtfully — a change made, or a disagreement explained — without defensiveness.
        levels:
          exemplary: Engages critique thoughtfully; fixes it, or explains a reasoned disagreement
          proficient: Responds adequately
          developing: Defensive or thin
          beginning: Ignores feedback
    looking_for:
      - "The portfolio is graded as a whole, on the writing itself. The counts (two bug reports, one feature request, and so on) are a floor for coverage, not the score. One careful bug report outweighs six rote ones."
      - "**Precision**, **completeness**, and **actionability** are one question asked three ways: could the person reading this act on it without coming back to you? That's the bar for every bug report and pull request you'll write at work, too."
      - "**Completeness** follows the bug-report anatomy from Session 15. For the feature request, it means a need and a user, not a solution order."
      - "**Responsiveness to feedback** is the two-way half of the skill. A disagreement handled well (\"I kept it because X; happy to revisit if Y\") counts as much as a fix."
      - "Your one- or two-sentence note on each artifact is read. It shows you can tell your best writing from your most recent."
    instructor_notes:
      - "Contents: 2 bug reports, 1 feature request, 1 PR description, 2 review comments, 2 responses to feedback — graded on **quality, not quantity**. The counts are a floor; a thoughtful bug report beats six rote ones."
      - "**Completeness** is anchored to the defect/fault/failure framing taught in Week 8 (reproduction, expected vs. actual)."
      - "**Responsiveness to feedback** assesses the two-way nature of technical communication — how the student receives and acts on review, not just how they write."

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------- CP10
  cp10:
    title: CP10 — Progress Demo + Repo Audit
    subtitle: "Team (or solo), with individual-contribution adjustment · Course weight: 16% (largest)"
    phase: 4
    publish: false
    criteria:
      - name: MVP plan quality
        weight: 15
        measures: "The one-pager: what's in / deferred / risk addressed / demo plan. (Graded from the {{ S20_DATE }} revision.)"
        levels:
          exemplary: "Sharp: what's in, what's deferred, what risk v1 addresses"
          proficient: Clear plan
          developing: Vague plan
          beginning: Missing
      - name: Proves key claims (vertical slice)
        weight: 25
        measures: The working slice clearly proves the project's core viability — the heaviest single criterion in the course.
        levels:
          exemplary: Working vertical slice clearly proves project viability
          proficient: Slice mostly proves it
          developing: Partial/broad-shallow
          beginning: Doesn't prove the point
      - name: Technical coherence
        weight: 15
        measures: Implementation clean and aligned with the design doc — the built thing matches the argued thing.
        levels:
          exemplary: Clean, coherent implementation aligned to the design
          proficient: Mostly coherent
          developing: Patchy
          beginning: Incoherent
      - name: Demo readiness
        weight: 15
        measures: A smooth, well-framed live demo (or a gracefully run fallback).
        levels:
          exemplary: Smooth, well-framed live demo
          proficient: Works with minor hiccups
          developing: Rough
          beginning: Fails to demo
      - name: Repo audit (peer onboarding)
        weight: 15
        measures: A stranger clones, builds, runs, and finds entry points from your docs alone.
        levels:
          exemplary: A peer clones, builds, runs, and finds entry points unaided
          proficient: Minor friction
          developing: Significant friction
          beginning: Cannot build/run
      - name: Documentation & honesty
        weight: 10
        measures: Limitations and known issues documented candidly — candor over oversell.
        levels:
          exemplary: Limitations and known issues documented candidly
          proficient: Documented
          developing: Thin
          beginning: Absent/overclaims
      - name: Individual contribution
        weight: 5
        measures: Git history and PRs show real, reasonably balanced contribution.
        levels:
          exemplary: Git history/PRs show real, balanced contribution
          proficient: Evident contribution
          developing: Uneven
          beginning: Little/none
    looking_for: []
    instructor_notes:
      - "**Proves key claims** is the heaviest single criterion in the course: an MVP's job is to *prove the project's core viability* via a vertical slice, not to be broad and shallow."
      - "**Repo audit** operationalizes contributor-readiness — a classmate must clone, build, run, and locate where a new feature would go. This is the buildability gate."
      - "**Documentation & honesty** rewards candor about limitations over polish that oversells."
      - "**Individual contribution** is where the team grade is adjusted by per-student git/PR evidence and peer evaluation (design §8)."

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------- CP11
  cp11:
    title: CP11 — Launch Package + Retrospective
    subtitle: Team (or solo) · Part of the 10% Final bundle, with CP12
    phase: 5
    publish: false
    criteria:
      - name: Launch-package completeness
        weight: 30
        measures: Architecture, roadmap, known-issues, tech-debt log, and onboarding all present and genuinely useful.
        levels:
          exemplary: Architecture overview, roadmap, known-issues, tech-debt log, onboarding all present and useful
          proficient: Mostly complete
          developing: Gaps
          beginning: Skeletal
      - name: Setup verification
        weight: 15
        measures: Setup independently reproducible from the docs — evidenced by the round-2 test sheet.
        levels:
          exemplary: Setup independently reproducible from docs
          proficient: Reproducible with minor friction
          developing: Unclear
          beginning: Fails
      - name: Retrospective honesty & insight
        weight: 25
        measures: Candid about what held, what the MVP validated, what's next — evidence over narrative, weighted heavily on purpose.
        levels:
          exemplary: Candid about what held, what the MVP validated, what's next
          proficient: Honest reflection
          developing: Surface-level
          beginning: Missing/spin
      - name: Future-work framing
        weight: 20
        measures: Compelling, realistic next steps without overselling — a roadmap a serious outside reader could believe (a potential contributor, a maintainer, a capstone faculty member).
        levels:
          exemplary: Compelling, realistic next steps without overselling
          proficient: Reasonable
          developing: Vague
          beginning: Absent or inflated
      - name: Contributor-readiness
        weight: 10
        measures: A new contributor could get up to speed and start building — the course's through-line, at the finish.
        levels:
          exemplary: A new contributor could get up to speed and start building
          proficient: Mostly
          developing: Partial
          beginning: "No"
    looking_for: []
    instructor_notes:
      - "**Launch-package completeness** and **Setup verification** assess whether a new contributor can actually get the project running and start building — the central deliverable of the course."
      - "**Retrospective honesty & insight** is weighted heavily because honest reflection (what assumptions held, what the MVP truly validated) is graded over a rosy narrative."
      - "**Future-work framing** rewards a credible roadmap that doesn't oversell — the lead may pitch this to capstone faculty."

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------- CP12
  cp12:
    title: CP12 — Final Pitch + Demo
    subtitle: Team (or solo) · Completes the 10% Final bundle · Defended at Demo Day (capstone faculty invited)
    phase: 5
    publish: false
    criteria:
      - name: '"What need does this serve?"'
        weight: 20
        measures: The need is vivid, real, and evidence-backed — the discovery arc, landed.
        levels:
          exemplary: Need is vivid, real, and evidence-backed
          proficient: Clear need
          developing: Vague
          beginning: Unconvincing
      - name: '"What has been built?" (live demo)'
        weight: 25
        measures: A confident live demo of a working slice — the heaviest criterion; slides can't earn it.
        levels:
          exemplary: Confident live demo of a working slice
          proficient: Working demo
          developing: Shaky/partial
          beginning: Fails
      - name: '"Where does it go next, and how can others join?"'
        weight: 20
        measures: A concrete, credible case for continuation — artifacts, not aspirations.
        levels:
          exemplary: Compelling, concrete case for how others can join and keep building
          proficient: Clear
          developing: Thin
          beginning: Missing
      - name: Integration
        weight: 15
        measures: Discovery → proposal → design → build → launch told as one coherent story.
        levels:
          exemplary: Ties discovery → proposal → design → build into one coherent story
          proficient: Mostly coherent
          developing: Disjointed
          beginning: No through-line
      - name: Persuasiveness & delivery
        weight: 10
        measures: Polished, well-paced, professional.
        levels:
          exemplary: Polished, persuasive, well-paced
          proficient: Solid
          developing: Uneven
          beginning: Poor
      - name: Q&A handling
        weight: 10
        measures: Direct, honest, composed — with faculty asking real questions.
        levels:
          exemplary: Direct, honest, composed with faculty
          proficient: Handles most
          developing: Struggles
          beginning: Cannot
    looking_for: []
    instructor_notes:
      - "The first three criteria are the **three questions the final must answer** (design §7, Week 14) — the spine of the mastery artifact, together carrying most of the weight."
      - "**Integration** rewards showing the whole founding arc as one story, which is what distinguishes a *founded, defensible, contributor-ready project* from strong code alone."
      - "**Q&A handling** matters because capstone faculty are in the room making carry-forward judgments."
